WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.448 --> 00:00:07.240
SCOTT ELLIS: Hello, my
name is Scott Ellis.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.560
And I'm a Product
Manager on Google Cloud.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.250
And today, we're going to talk
about managing sensitive data

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.350
in hybrid environments.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.690
What we're going to cover
today is a quick overview

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:20.133
of our Cloud data loss
prevention or DLP platform.

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:21.550
And then we're
going to go and see

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:24.640
how we can use DLP in
hybrid environments.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.290
And after that, we're going
to show a demo of this

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:32.049
by using DLP to inspect data
into MySQL database that's

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:33.740
running in a virtual machine.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.910
And then we're going to migrate
some of that data into BigQuery

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.050
and use DLP again to do data
masking or data redaction.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.040
Let's get started.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.690
So first an overview of our
data loss prevention platform.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.760
First we feel that
protecting sensitive data

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.970
really begins with knowing
where that data exists.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:56.000
And so one of the core
features of our platform

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.317
is discovery and classification.

00:00:58.317 --> 00:00:59.900
And our data loss
prevention platform,

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.790
again, is fully
managed service that

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:03.860
helps you discover,
classify, and protect

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.260
your most sensitive data.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.320
And we do this, again, through
discovery, classification,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.260
as well as a set of tools
called de-identification,

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:16.560
which is a way to transform
or mask sensitive data.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.050
And this platform
is built to work

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.900
on structured data,
unstructured data,

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.390
as well as workloads that
might be in real time,

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:25.730
or at rest or in batch.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:31.420
To cover this in more detail,
when we talk about discovery

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.940
and classification of
sensitive data, what we mean

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:37.450
is being able to look at data
and identify different elements

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.060
inside that data that
might be sensitive.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.980
An example of that
could be things

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:45.040
like personally identifiable
information or PII.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.640
This would be things like names,
dates of birth, credit card

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.670
numbers, and a variety
of different things.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:54.790
And we have different
types out of the box

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.130
that we support that
are both global,

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.620
as well as country
specific identifiers.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.570
We also have some
special categories

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.940
like credentials and
secrets that help look

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.850
for things like
passwords or off tokens

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.080
whether it be on our
cloud or other clouds.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.860
And these are things that
might not identify a person,

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:15.370
but are sensitive and
might give a credential

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:19.060
or access to privileged
access to resources.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.980
You can also
customize all of this

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.180
either by defining your
own info type detectors

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.960
or by using detection rules to
change how our detectors work

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.010
or to tune them for your needs.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.262
That second set of
capabilities, again, we

00:02:34.262 --> 00:02:35.220
call de-identification.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.520
It's often referred to as
things like data masking

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:39.850
or tokenization.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:42.810
And this is where we go
in and actually manipulate

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:46.500
the data to try to de-risk
it or obfuscate it somehow.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.440
And as you can see in this
before-and-after animation,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.840
what we're doing is we're
applying those techniques

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.090
to different columns
in this table.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.560
In the first three
columns, we are

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.570
applying a technique
that basically

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.620
transforms the entire column.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.070
And then in that fourth
column, the comments field,

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.800
we're actually using a
combination of our inspection

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.260
and our masking to look
for data inside of the cell

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:10.840
and only redact that item.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:14.070
So you see in that first row, we
actually find an email address.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:17.730
Instead of removing the entire
cell or the entire comment,

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.140
we just remove
that email address

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.770
and leave the rest intact.

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.720
So that's an example how
we combine our inspection

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.660
and our de-identification
to work on unstructured data

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:27.660
or semi structured data.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:34.500
And where this may be
applicable or where

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.540
you might use this,
one example, is

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.750
to help prevent exposure of
raw secrets or sensitive data.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.640
So imagine, again, you had,
let's say, your public website

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.560
or your company website, and
all of those public assets

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.260
were hosted in a
Cloud storage bucket.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.420
And these are assets
that power your website

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.700
that are generally
available to anyone when

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:54.990
they're viewing your website.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:57.060
But what happens if
somebody uploads something

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.660
into that bucket or into that
folder that shouldn't be there?

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:02.910
Maybe it's sensitive.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.850
So running something like an
inspection scan of that bucket,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:09.450
we could identify, for example,
here if somebody uploaded a GCP

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.850
credential into that bucket,
which again, you would not

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.310
want to expose that publicly
as it could give [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.040
access to some of
your resources.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.769
Another example would
be to unblock workloads.

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:22.280
Maybe it's a machine learning
or artificial intelligence

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:26.160
workload, or some sort of
a cloud migration workload.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.470
And here we would
look at moving data

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.390
into those different
environments

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.040
and applying that
de-identification

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.528
to obscure or mask
the data, but still

00:04:34.528 --> 00:04:36.820
allow that analysis or that
machine learning to happen.

00:04:39.098 --> 00:04:40.640
Another use case
would be really just

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.920
to understand and classify data
across your entire enterprise.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:48.080
And our cloud platform
has an API arrest

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.960
or a gRPC API that
enables you to call it

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.490
from virtually anywhere.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:54.948
And that's what we're going to
focus on in the next section

00:04:54.948 --> 00:04:57.840
is how we can use this
in hybrid environments

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:03.310
that may be on or off Cloud
or on or off Google Cloud.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:04.310
And let's get into that.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:07.335
So what do we actually mean
by hybrid environments?

00:05:07.335 --> 00:05:08.960
Well, really, we're
thinking of, again,

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.270
taking those core capabilities
around inspection,

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:15.200
classification, and
masking, and applying it

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.710
on data that might be on other
Clouds, might be on premise,

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:20.910
could be in non-native
storage systems.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.243
This could be like a database
that you're running inside

00:05:23.243 --> 00:05:24.328
of a virtual machine.

00:05:24.328 --> 00:05:26.120
Maybe it's running
inside of an application

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:28.100
like a web or a mobile app.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:32.210
Or it could, of course, be
running on Google Cloud.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:33.440
So a few examples of that.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:34.982
Maybe you want to
inspect data that's

00:05:34.982 --> 00:05:39.260
running in something
like AWS's RDS system,

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:41.210
or maybe it's in MySQL
database running inside

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.760
of a virtual machine, or
again, a database that's

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:44.840
running on premise.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:48.560
We're actually going to
show a demo of this later,

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.808
looking at MySQL running
in a virtual machine.

00:05:51.808 --> 00:05:53.600
Maybe you want to
inspect and tokenize data

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.490
as you're migrating
that data from on prem

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:58.340
into Cloud or between
environments like production,

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:00.440
development, and analytics.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:02.840
Another example could be
inspecting and redacting

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:06.200
transactions from a web
or mobile application

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:10.010
before you store
that data at rest.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:12.720
So to do this, we're going to
talk about two of our methods.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.490
These are two methods on
our Cloud DLP platform.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:19.390
One called content methods
and one called hybrid method.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:21.080
Now hybrid methods
are a newer method

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.693
that we've released recently.

00:06:23.693 --> 00:06:25.360
But let's go over
both of these methods.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:26.755
So first content methods.

00:06:26.755 --> 00:06:28.130
You'll notice
actually that these

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.680
are pretty similar as
far as the data sources

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.510
that they apply to.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:34.220
This could be data
running on any cloud,

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.740
on premise, and virtually
any data source.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:38.690
But with content
methods, it's what

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:43.380
we call a synchronous
request and response.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:45.440
These are methods where
you make a request.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.420
You send data in that request.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.150
And you get a response
back in that request.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:52.430
And that response is the answer.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:55.440
It's the classification
or the mass data.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.270
These methods are
stateless and they

00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:00.920
allow both inspection and
de-identification capabilities.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.435
Now our new hybrid
methods are similar

00:07:03.435 --> 00:07:04.560
but a little bit different.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.130
What they do is they
actually take your data.

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:09.350
They allow you to
do a fire and forget

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:11.882
where you can send this
data in for inspection

00:07:11.882 --> 00:07:12.590
but then move on.

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:14.215
You don't have to
wait for the response

00:07:14.215 --> 00:07:15.700
or process the response.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:19.700
In here, DLP will
aggregate that data

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:22.310
and put it together
and aggregate it

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.740
so that you can store
that data or analyze it,

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:26.870
for example, in
something like BigQuery

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:29.420
or make reports of
Data Studio, or publish

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:30.890
other types of alerts.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.387
And for now, this is
only for inspection.

00:07:33.387 --> 00:07:35.720
And that's what we're going
to talk about later and demo

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.840
this later as well, how we
can take information, send it

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:41.240
through these hybrid methods,
and then produce an audit

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.160
report using BigQuery
and Data Studio.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:44.660
But again, these
are the two methods

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:50.030
you would use to handle
data in hybrid environments.

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:52.190
And here's an example
of what that audit

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:53.337
report might look like.

00:07:53.337 --> 00:07:54.920
So again, imagine
you're scanning data

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:57.140
across several
different assets and you

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:59.480
want to collect all
of this in one place,

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:02.750
analyze it using
BigQuery or visualize it

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.870
in some sort of audit report
like we're showing here, using

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:07.280
something like Data Studio.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:09.650
In this example, we're showing
several different things

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:10.538
that we've inspected.

00:08:10.538 --> 00:08:12.080
And as you see,
we're getting results

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:13.850
about what kind of
database we looked

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:20.440
at, what tables and columns
and DLP findings we have there.

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:25.420
Now, another aspect to this is
what we call regionalization.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:28.540
And with DLP, you also
have regional endpoints.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:31.020
And this means that you
can actually specify where

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:32.890
your data is being processed.

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:35.490
So for example, let's say
you want to inspect data

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:36.355
from on premise.

00:08:36.355 --> 00:08:38.730
But you want to make sure that
the inspection part that's

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:41.130
running on our
platform is limited

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.320
to a particular location.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.840
Maybe you want all your
data to stay in Australia

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:47.640
or in Europe or in the US.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.950
You can specify a
regional endpoint,

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:52.720
which means that your data will
be processed in that region.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.012
And it gives you the control
of whether you're doing it

00:08:55.012 --> 00:08:57.570
for data residency
purposes or you're

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:02.280
planning out an availability
or a performance or latency

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:03.060
requirement.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:07.130
You can pick those regional
endpoints if you need to.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:09.530
Great, now let's go in and go
a little bit deeper on what

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:11.220
these hybrid methods look like.

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.980
So when we use the new
hybrid method, what we do

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:17.960
is we created a hybrid job.

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:20.420
In this job, it really
is an active listener

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.220
that's going to be
listening for requests

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:25.010
and then aggregating all
those findings for you

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:28.340
so that you can come back
later and see the results.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:29.960
And when we define
this request, we're

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.585
going to basically create a job
instead of specifying a storage

00:09:32.585 --> 00:09:34.940
location like Cloud
storage or BigQuery.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:37.520
We would pick a storage
location of hybrid.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:40.318
We would then provide additional
metadata for that job.

00:09:40.318 --> 00:09:42.860
Now this metadata is important
because that's the information

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.420
that we're going to use to
aggregate everything together.

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:48.830
You can specify some of
that in the actual job

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.810
or in each request.

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:54.020
And let's go into a little
bit more detail of what

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:55.920
a request might look like.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:58.270
So here is an actual
code sample where

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:00.740
we're showing what
a hybrid inspection

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.342
request would look like.

00:10:02.342 --> 00:10:03.800
On the right side,
we're actually--

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:06.970
this is a code sample in Java.

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:08.660
And what we're showing
here at the top

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:11.330
is there's some content
that we want to inspect.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:13.740
In this case, we're going to
be inspecting tabular data.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:17.310
So we've provided headers
and rows of that data.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:19.790
Then we have our hybrid,
our actual hybrid request.

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.680
In this case, that's
where we're supplying

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.380
all of those details
and all that metadata

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:25.850
that we want to
aggregate around.

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:29.210
So here's where we would pass
it things like a MySQL database

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:33.590
type or a version, or maybe
a database name or table name

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:37.690
because we want to add those
things to our report later on.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:39.040
And then we send the request.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:40.885
And in this example
again, this is just

00:10:40.885 --> 00:10:42.510
showing the snippet
that will basically

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:45.930
provide all that information
and send that request in.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.670
And then on the server side,
DLP will inspect that data,

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:52.380
aggregate it together, and allow
you to then go in and analyze

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:55.350
that information using something
like the BigQuery or Data

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:57.780
Studio.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.300
Great, now that we've shown
you what a request looks like,

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:02.190
let's move into an
actual demo of this.

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:03.960
And what we're going
to show in this demo

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.820
is we're going to
scan data that's

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:08.040
running in a MySQL
database that is

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:10.370
running on a virtual machine.

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:11.885
We're going to
inspect that data.

00:11:11.885 --> 00:11:14.010
And then we're going to
migrate one of those tables

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:17.940
over to BigQuery, using
DLP again to do masking.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:19.950
So we basically can
protect that data

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:22.560
as we're migrating it
from this virtual machine

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:25.370
into a managed
service like BigQuery.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:25.870
Great.

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:28.000
Let's get started on the demo.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:31.060
We're going to show
a demo in two parts.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:32.920
In part one, we're
going to inspect data

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:37.595
from a MySQL database that's
running in a virtual machine.

00:11:37.595 --> 00:11:39.970
And to do this, we're going
to use a custom script that's

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:44.080
going to read this information,
send it into our Cloud data

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.540
loss prevention platform,
using the Hybrid inspection

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:50.290
method, which is going to
then write data into BigQuery.

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:54.650
And we're going to visualize
that in Data Studio.

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:58.640
The second part, we're going to
migrate a table from that MySQL

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:03.140
database using Cloud DLP
and Dataflow to tokenize

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:06.860
that data as we move
it into BigQuery

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:08.695
and migrate it from
MySQL into BigQuery.

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:12.360
Let's get started.

00:12:14.723 --> 00:12:16.390
Great, the first thing
we're going to do

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:18.250
is create a new hybrid job.

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:22.060
To do that, I've navigated to
security and then Data Loss

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:24.700
Prevention in the Cloud console.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:26.850
And now I'm going
to click on Create

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:31.310
and then Job, provide
a name for the job,

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:34.910
and then pick a storage
location of Hybrid.

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:36.650
Now Hybrid, again,
means that it's going

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:38.210
to create an active listener.

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:40.892
It's going to be listening
for request streams.

00:12:40.892 --> 00:12:42.350
Each of those
requests streams will

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:45.830
include the content
that we want to inspect

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:48.750
as well as any labels
that act as metadata.

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:51.320
So again, these labels can
define the type of data

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:53.870
that we're scanning,
maybe a database.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:57.560
And our demo will be a MySQL
database, the database name,

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:59.130
and the table name.

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:01.470
Now we can put
require labels here,

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:03.710
which means every
request is required

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:05.660
to have that label
filled out, as well

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:08.750
as optional labels that we
can apply it to job level,

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:10.610
but that can be overridden
by each request.

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:15.990
Now the rest of this
job configuration

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:17.670
is similar to other job types.

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:20.070
I define the types of things
that I want to look for,

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:23.760
either by using a template or
by putting the configuration

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:26.390
as I have here in the job.

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:29.160
Then when I'm done, I
simply click create.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:34.590
Great, now that we have our
job, we're ready to use it.

