00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:56.565
As we see here, it's
pulling for the input file.

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:07.780
It looks like we
processed some files here.

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:09.220
We've run some tokenization.

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:11.665
I'm going to go over
to BigQuery here.

00:21:11.665 --> 00:21:12.960
Let's just refresh this.

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:14.710
So we can pull up that
data set that we're

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:17.050
writing the results to.

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:19.441
And let's see if it's there yet.

00:21:19.441 --> 00:21:22.520
OK, great, so you see
here we have a CARs

00:21:22.520 --> 00:21:26.140
table that's just been created.

00:21:26.140 --> 00:21:27.830
Let's just go look
at a preview here.

00:21:27.830 --> 00:21:32.430
And as you can see, the email
address and the VIN number

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:38.550
have both been obfuscated
and basically identified.

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:40.830
So that we're protecting
that information.

00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:43.620
What this means is someone
can now analyze this data

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:47.100
that we've extracted and moved
over from that MySQL database

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:49.300
without revealing
the email address.

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:50.490
But they can still do joins.

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:52.320
They can still do
aggregate because we've

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:56.640
chosen to use a deterministic
tokenization method that

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:57.930
preserves that integrity.

00:21:57.930 --> 00:22:00.825
So again, the same email
gets the same token.

00:22:00.825 --> 00:22:03.960
The same VIN number
gets the same token,

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:07.410
but we're not revealing the
underlying sensitive data.

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:09.830
And this concludes the
